            <ArticleTitle>Pain during the labor active phase: the effect of walking.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1157-62</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="UNLABELLED">This study aimed to verify whether the distance walked is correlated with women's pain level throughout the active phase of labor.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODOLOGY" NlmCategory="METHODS">We realized an analytic, quasi-experimental intervention study. Study participants were 80 primiparous parturient women, who were admitted during spontaneous labor, with 37-42 weeks, at the start of the active phase.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA ANALYSIS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Spearman's correlation test.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">the parturient women walked an average distance of 1,624 meters, 63.09% of the active phase of labor and during an average time of five hours. Pain scores increase along with the advance in cervical dilatation. However, we only found a significant positive correlation when 5cm of dilatation had been reached, that is, the more distance the participants walked, the higher the pain scores they reached.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
